---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import NewsletterCallout from '../../components/NewsletterCallout.astro';
import { articleSchema, faqSchema } from '../../lib/article';
import { PITCHES, SR } from '../../data/sr-stats';

const f = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const T = PITCHES.timing;
const [founders, marketing] = PITCHES.titles;

const title = 'Sponsorship Email Template and Subject Lines (2026)';
const description = 'Copy-paste sponsorship email templates: a cold pitch, a CEO email with a media kit and a request email, plus subject lines and who to send them to.';
const h1 = 'Sponsorship Email Template: How to Cold Email Brands for a Sponsorship';
const path = '/blog/cold-email-sponsorship-pitch/';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a good sponsorship email subject line?',
    a: 'Name the brand and say what the email is, in plain words. For example: "[Brand] x [Channel]: sponsorship idea" or "Sponsorship idea for [Brand] (media kit attached)". Avoid vague lines like "Collaboration opportunity" that say nothing about who you are or what you want.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who should I send a sponsorship email to?',
    a: `Send it to whoever owns the sponsorship budget at the brand, using the contact you can find rather than waiting for a perfect job title. In Book a Sponsor's pitches, founders, CEOs and owners replied at ${founders.rate} (${f(founders.replies)} of ${f(founders.sent)}) and marketing titles at ${marketing.rate} (${f(marketing.replies)} of ${f(marketing.sent)}), about the same.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I write an email to a CEO with a sponsorship attachment?',
    a: 'Keep the body to a few lines. Say who you are and who reads you, make one specific offer, attach a short media kit as a PDF and say what is inside it, and ask one question: does the CEO handle sponsorships, or should you write to someone else?',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I write a sponsorship request email?',
    a: 'State what you want sponsored, what the brand gets, what it costs and what happens next, in that order and in a few lines. The sponsorship request template on this page does exactly that.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I reach out to companies for sponsorship?',
    a: 'Start with brands that already pay to reach audiences like yours, find the person who owns the budget, and send a short email with one specific offer and your media kit. Follow up once or twice. For a YouTube sponsorship, make the offer a video integration and use the same structure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast do brands reply to a sponsorship email?',
    a: `Fast, when they reply at all. In Book a Sponsor's automated pitches, the median reply came ${T.medianHours} hours after the send, ${T.within24h} of replies arrived within 24 hours and ${T.within7d} within 7 days (${T.n} replies).`,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  schema={[articleSchema({ headline: h1, description, path, published: '2026-01-24', modified: '2026-10-02' }), faqSchema(faq)]}
  articleDate="2026-01-24"
  articleModified="2026-10-02"
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 md:py-20">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900">{h1}</h1>
      <div class="mt-2"><Byline published="2026-01-24" modified="2026-10-02" /></div>
      <NewsletterCallout />
    </header>
    <div class="mt-8 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p><strong>A sponsorship email works when it is short and specific: who you are, who reads you, one offer and one question. Send it to whoever owns the budget at the brand. In Book a Sponsor's automated outreach for newsletters, {PITCHES.replyRate} of {f(PITCHES.sent)} first pitches got a reply, so write one good email and send it to many brands.</strong></p>

      <p>That {PITCHES.replyRate} is Book a Sponsor's read of its own pitches: {f(PITCHES.sent)} first emails to {f(PITCHES.brands)} brands for {PITCHES.newsletters} newsletters, {PITCHES.window}, read {PITCHES.read}. They were automated, not hand-written. A "yes" ({PITCHES.yeses} of them) means the brand's first answer was interested. It does not mean a signed deal.</p>

      <h2>What should a sponsorship email say?</h2>

      <p>A sponsorship email says four things: who you are and who reads you, why this brand fits, one specific offer, and one question to answer. Everything else belongs in your media kit.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Who you are and who reads you.</strong> One sentence with your audience size and the type of reader, using a number you can back up from your own analytics.</li>
        <li><strong>Why this brand.</strong> One line that shows you looked: a product, a launch or a campaign you actually saw. Skip "I love your brand."</li>
        <li><strong>One specific offer.</strong> A placement, a format and a date, such as a sponsored slot in the [month] issue. "Let's collaborate" gives them nothing to say yes to.</li>
        <li><strong>One question.</strong> "Would you like the media kit?" or "Who handles sponsorships?" is easier to answer than "Let me know what you think."</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Put the audience data, past results and rates in the media kit and attach or link it. The <a href="/blog/sponsorship-media-kit-guide/">sponsorship media kit guide</a> covers what goes in it.</p>

      <h2>Sponsorship email template for a cold pitch</h2>

      <p>Use this template when you have never written to the brand before. Fill every bracket with something true, and delete any sentence you can't fill.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Subject:</strong> [Brand] &times; [Channel]: sponsorship idea</p>
        <p>Hi [First name],</p>
        <p>[One specific thing you saw from the brand: a product, a launch, a campaign.]</p>
        <p>I run [Channel], a [newsletter / channel / podcast] about [niche], read by [who they are] ([audience size]).</p>
        <p>I'd like to feature [Product] in [specific placement and date]. [One sentence on why it fits your readers.]</p>
        <p>My media kit has the audience data and rates: [link]. Would you like me to send details for [month]?</p>
        <p>Thanks,<br />[Your name]</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>What is a good sponsorship email subject line?</h2>

      <p>A good sponsorship email subject line names the brand or the offer and says what the email is, in plain words, so the reader knows at a glance it is a sponsorship idea and not a mass mailing. This page has no open-rate data for subject lines, so the patterns below are examples to adapt, not a ranking.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Pattern</th>
            <th>Example subject line</th>
            <th>Use it when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Brand and idea</td>
            <td>[Brand] &times; [Channel]: sponsorship idea</td>
            <td>Any cold pitch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Attachment</td>
            <td>Sponsorship idea for [Brand] (media kit attached)</td>
            <td>You attach the kit</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Request</td>
            <td>Sponsorship request: [Channel] &times; [Brand]</td>
            <td>You are asking for a specific package</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Open date</td>
            <td>A [month] slot in [Channel] for [Product]</td>
            <td>You have a date to fill</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Existing use</td>
            <td>I already recommend [Product]. Make it official?</td>
            <td>It is true</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Referral</td>
            <td>[Name] suggested I write to you</td>
            <td>Someone really did</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Skip subject lines that say nothing about you or the offer, such as "Collaboration opportunity" or "Quick question". Don't start with "Re:" or "Fwd:" to look like an existing thread. Write the subject after the body and check it matches: if it says "media kit attached", attach the kit.</p>

      <h2>How do you email a CEO a sponsorship pitch with a media kit attached?</h2>

      <p>Keep the email to a few lines, say what you are offering in the first two sentences, attach the media kit as a PDF, and ask if the CEO handles sponsorships or if you should write to someone else.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Sponsorship idea for [Brand] (media kit attached)</p>
        <p>Hi [First name],</p>
        <p>I'm [Your name]. I run [Channel], which reaches [audience size] [who they are]. I think [Brand] fits them because [one reason].</p>
        <p>I've attached my media kit: audience, past results and rates. The offer I'd start with is [placement] on [date] for [price, or "the price is in the kit"].</p>
        <p>Do you handle sponsorships yourself, or should I send this to someone on your team?</p>
        <p>Thanks,<br />[Your name]</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Say what is in the attachment, as the template does, so a stranger knows why to open it. Add a link to the same kit under your signature in case the attachment doesn't come through.</p>

      <h2>What does a sponsorship request email look like?</h2>

      <p>A sponsorship request email states what you want sponsored, what the brand gets, what it costs and what happens next, in that order.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Sponsorship request: [Channel] &times; [Brand]</p>
        <p>Hi [First name],</p>
        <p>I'm asking [Brand] to sponsor [what: the [month] issues, the [date] episode, a video].</p>
        <p>What you get: [placement, format and link], in front of [audience size and who they are].</p>
        <p>What it costs: [price] for [package]. If your budget is different, tell me and I'll adjust the package.</p>
        <p>If this is a yes, reply with a start date and I'll hold the slot. If someone else decides, who should I write to?</p>
        <p>Thanks,<br />[Your name]</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Asking a company to sponsor an event or a project instead? Use the same four lines and swap the deliverable for what the sponsor gets, such as their name on the program or a booth. For the price line, see <a href="/blog/how-much-to-charge-for-sponsorship/">how much to charge for a sponsorship</a> and <a href="/blog/sponsorship-packages/">how to build sponsorship packages</a>.</p>

      <h2>If you already use the product, say so first</h2>

      <p>If you already use the product and have mentioned it to your audience, lead with that. It is the strongest reason to write to that brand, and you can point to where you said it. Use this template only if it is true.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Subject:</strong> I already recommend [Product]. Make it official?</p>
        <p>Hi [First name],</p>
        <p>I've used [Product] for [how long] and mentioned it to my audience in [where and when: link].</p>
        <p>I run [Channel], read by [who they are] ([audience size]). I'd like to turn that into a paid, labeled sponsorship: [placement and date].</p>
        <p>My media kit is here: [link]. Is that something [Brand] does?</p>
        <p>Thanks,<br />[Your name]</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Who to email for a sponsorship: the founder or the marketing team?</h2>

      <p>Email whoever owns the sponsorship budget at the brand, using the person you can find. Job title made no real difference in Book a Sponsor's pitches: founders, CEOs and owners replied at {founders.rate} ({f(founders.replies)} of {f(founders.sent)} pitches) and marketing titles at {marketing.rate} ({f(marketing.replies)} of {f(marketing.sent)}). The rest went to other titles and are not compared here. Source: Book a Sponsor's {f(PITCHES.sent)} pitches to brands, {PITCHES.window}.</p>

      <p>That means you should not spend an hour hunting for the perfect title. At a small brand there may be no marketing team, so write to the founder. At a larger brand, write to whoever runs partnerships or marketing. Titles worth searching for are Brand Partnerships Manager, Influencer Marketing Manager, Creator Relations Manager and Head of Marketing.</p>

      <p>Search LinkedIn for the company name plus one of those titles, then find the address with an email finder such as Hunter or Apollo. If you can't find a name, write to the founder and ask who handles sponsorships, as the CEO template above does. A shared inbox like info@ has no owner for your pitch. To find which brands to write to in the first place, see <a href="/blog/find-brands-that-sponsor/">how to find brands that sponsor</a>.</p>

      <h2>How fast do brands reply to a sponsorship email?</h2>

      <p>Fast, when they reply at all. Across {T.n} brand replies, timed from Book a Sponsor's automated first pitch to the brand's first answer, the median reply came {T.medianHours} hours after the send. {T.within24h} of replies arrived within 24 hours, {T.within3d} within 3 days and {T.within7d} within 7 days (Book a Sponsor's {f(PITCHES.sent)} pitches, {PITCHES.window}). A brand that has not answered after about a week is unlikely to answer the first email, so that is the point to follow up. What to send is covered in <a href="/blog/sponsorship-follow-up-email/">sponsorship follow-up email templates and timing</a>.</p>

      <h2>How should you follow up on a sponsorship email?</h2>

      <p>Follow up once or twice, in the same thread, starting 3 to 7 days after the first email. Add one new fact or one easier question each time, and stop after the second. The timing table, five follow-up templates and the stop rule are on the <a href="/blog/sponsorship-follow-up-email/">sponsorship follow-up email page</a>.</p>

      <h2>What should you do when a brand replies?</h2>

      <p>Reply the same working day. Thank them, attach the media kit if you haven't already, offer two or three times for a call, and ask whether they have goals or a timeline you should know about. On the call, ask what they want the sponsorship to do (awareness, signups or sales), what budget they have and when it should run, then fit your proposal to that.</p>

      <h2>How many sponsorship emails do you need to send?</h2>

      <p>More than you expect. Book a Sponsor's automated pitches took about {PITCHES.pitchesPerYes} first pitches per yes ({PITCHES.yeses} yeses from {f(PITCHES.sent)} pitches), and a yes is not yet a paid deal. The full funnel and the arithmetic for your own goal are in <a href="/blog/how-many-brands-to-pitch/">how many brands to pitch</a>. Keep a sheet of every pitch with the brand, contact, date sent, reply and next follow-up date.</p>

      <p>If you run a newsletter, <a href={SR.cta('cold-email-sponsorship-pitch')}>Book a Sponsor</a> finds brands that sponsored similar newsletters and sends the pitches and follow-ups from its own mailboxes, so your time goes to the newsletter instead of the inbox.</p>

      <h2>Sponsorship email questions</h2>

      {faq.map((item) => (
        <>
          <h3>{item.q}</h3>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </>
      ))}

    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
